      {
        "id": "E2",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Improve TTFT for multi-turn agentic workloads with bi-directional KV transfer in vLLM",
        "sourceUrl": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/improve-ttft-for-multi-turn-agentic-workloads-with-bi-directional-kv-transfer-in-vllm/",
        "source": "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-10-19T16:03:23.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-10-19T16:58:55.064Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E3",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "In this post, we show how bi-directional KV cache transfer lets prefill and decode nodes reuse previously computed context across turns, which improves accelerator utilization and helps applications sustain responsive TTFT as conversations grow.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/improve-ttft-for-multi-turn-agentic-workloads-with-bi-directional-kv-transfer-in-vllm/",
        "source": "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-10-19T16:03:23.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-10-19T16:58:55.064Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E4",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "In our multi-turn benchmarks on a 1P1D deployment using Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) P5en instances, bi-directional KV transfer reduced TTFT by up to 1.6x for Qwen3-32B and up to 3.3x for zai-org/GLM-5-FP8 (see the Performance data section).",
        "sourceUrl": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/improve-ttft-for-multi-turn-agentic-workloads-with-bi-directional-kv-transfer-in-vllm/",
        "source": "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-10-19T16:03:23.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-10-19T16:58:55.064Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E5",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Bi-directional KV transfer is a mechanism in which a decode node returns previously computed KV to a prefill node on subsequent turns of a conversation.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/improve-ttft-for-multi-turn-agentic-workloads-with-bi-directional-kv-transfer-in-vllm/",
        "source": "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-10-19T16:03:23.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-10-19T16:58:55.064Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E6",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "The numbered red path illustrates the bi-directional KV transfer additions: (1) decode node retains eligible KV blocks after generation, (2) returns their metadata in the streaming response, and (3) the router caches that metadata for the next prefill request.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/improve-ttft-for-multi-turn-agentic-workloads-with-bi-directional-kv-transfer-in-vllm/",
        "source": "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-10-19T16:03:23.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-10-19T16:58:55.064Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E7",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Figure 1: Standard prefill-to-decode flow (blue) and the bi-directional KV transfer additions (red)",
        "sourceUrl": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/improve-ttft-for-multi-turn-agentic-workloads-with-bi-directional-kv-transfer-in-vllm/",
        "source": "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-10-19T16:03:23.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-10-19T16:58:55.064Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E8",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "vLLM includes connector-side support for bi-directional KV transfer, while coordinating KV reuse across conversation turns requires a KV-cache-aware router. vLLM GitHub provides an example proxy that implements the flow used in this post which can be adapted for your environment.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/improve-ttft-for-multi-turn-agentic-workloads-with-bi-directional-kv-transfer-in-vllm/",
        "source": "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-10-19T16:03:23.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-10-19T16:58:55.064Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E9",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Under bi-directional KV transfer mode, it holds them for a configurable lifetime ( decoder_kv_blocks_ttl , default 480 seconds) and publishes their location and expiry.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/improve-ttft-for-multi-turn-agentic-workloads-with-bi-directional-kv-transfer-in-vllm/",
        "source": "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-10-19T16:03:23.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-10-19T16:58:55.064Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E10",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Across both the models, turning on bi-directional KV transfer reduces prefill computation on multi-turn conversations, and the benefit typically grows with prompt length.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/improve-ttft-for-multi-turn-agentic-workloads-with-bi-directional-kv-transfer-in-vllm/",
        "source": "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-10-19T16:03:23.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-10-19T16:58:55.064Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E11",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "The following chart plots TTFT against prompt length for Qwen3-32B, comparing bi-directional KV transfer ON versus OFF.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/improve-ttft-for-multi-turn-agentic-workloads-with-bi-directional-kv-transfer-in-vllm/",
        "source": "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-10-19T16:03:23.000Z",
